---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import EcosystemSim from '../../simulators/ecosystem/EcosystemSim.astro';
import { ecosystemQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/ecosystem';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'Energy flows, matter cycles' },
  { id: 'roles', label: 'Producers, consumers and decomposers' },
  { id: 'chains', label: 'Food chains and food webs' },
  { id: 'energy', label: 'Energy transfer between levels' },
  { id: 'pyramids', label: 'Pyramids of energy' },
  { id: 'carbon', label: 'The carbon cycle' },
  { id: 'nitrogen', label: 'The nitrogen cycle' },
  { id: 'changes', label: 'Adding and removing organisms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Chapter 46: Ecosystems (Energy Flow through Ecosystems; Biogeochemical Cycles). OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-introduction' },
  { text: 'Lindeman, R. L. (1942). The trophic-dynamic aspect of ecology. Ecology, 23(4), 399–417.' },
  { text: 'Pauly, D., & Christensen, V. (1995). Primary production required to sustain global fisheries. Nature, 374, 255–257.' },
  { text: 'Galloway, J. N., et al. (2004). Nitrogen cycles: past, present, and future. Biogeochemistry, 70, 153–226.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="ecosystem-energy-flow"
  description="Interactive ecosystem simulator: follow energy from the Sun through a meadow food web, watch it lost as heat at every level, see decomposers recycle nutrients, and explore the carbon and nitrogen cycles."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={ecosystemQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A meadow runs on sunlight. Plants capture it, rabbits and grasshoppers eat the plants, foxes and frogs eat them, and
      fungi and bacteria clean up whatever dies. Watch energy flow through this food web and drain away as heat, while the
      same carbon and nitrogen atoms go round and round. Then remove a species and see what happens.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <EcosystemSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Press <strong>Start</strong>. In <strong>Food web and energy</strong>, yellow dots are energy in food, red wisps are heat, brown dots are dead matter and green dots are recycled nutrients. Use <strong>Show</strong> to see energy and matter separately.</li>
      <li><strong>Click</strong> any organism to see its role and what it eats. Watch the <strong>energy pyramid</strong> and the readouts for how much energy reaches each level.</li>
      <li>Choose an organism and press <strong>Remove all</strong>, <strong>Halve</strong>, <strong>Double / add</strong> or <strong>Restore</strong>, or try a scenario such as <strong>No decomposers</strong>. Follow the populations on the graph.</li>
      <li>Change <strong>Sunlight</strong> to see how the energy input limits the whole ecosystem.</li>
      <li>Switch to <strong>Nutrient cycles</strong> to follow carbon or nitrogen atoms, and click each process.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">Energy flows, matter cycles</h2>
  <p>
    Two things move through every ecosystem: <strong>energy</strong> and <strong>matter</strong>. Energy arrives as
    sunlight, is passed along food chains in chemical form, and eventually leaves as heat. It cannot be recycled, so
    ecosystems need a constant supply. Matter, the atoms of carbon, nitrogen and other elements, is not lost: it is used
    again and again as it moves between the air, soil, water and living things. This is <strong>nutrient
    cycling</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roles">Producers, consumers and decomposers</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Producers</strong>, mainly plants and algae, capture light energy by photosynthesis and build it into
    sugars. <strong>Consumers</strong> obtain energy by eating: <strong>herbivores</strong> (primary consumers) eat
    producers, and <strong>carnivores</strong> (secondary and tertiary consumers) eat other animals.
    <strong>Decomposers</strong>, mostly fungi and bacteria, feed on dead organisms and waste. They are essential, because
    they release the nutrients locked in dead matter back into the soil and air.
  </p>

  <h2 id="chains">Food chains and food webs</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>food chain</strong> shows one path of feeding, such as plants → rabbits → foxes. The arrows point in the
    direction energy moves, from the eaten to the eater. Real ecosystems contain many linked chains, forming a
    <strong>food web</strong>. Each feeding step is a <strong>trophic level</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="energy">Energy transfer between levels</h2>
  <p>
    Only a small part of the energy at one level becomes new biomass at the next. Some of the plant is never eaten, some
    of what is eaten is not digested and leaves as waste, and most of the energy that is absorbed is used for respiration
    and lost as <strong>heat</strong>. Typically about 5–20% passes on; the often-quoted “10% rule” is a rough
    average, not a law. In the simulator the efficiencies are not fixed: they come out of the model and change as
    populations change. This is why food chains rarely have more than four or five levels.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pyramids">Pyramids of energy</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>pyramid of energy</strong> shows how much energy each trophic level stores per unit of time. It is always
    wider at the bottom, because energy is lost at every step. Pyramids of numbers or biomass can sometimes be inverted
    (one tree can feed thousands of insects), but a pyramid of energy cannot.
  </p>

  <h2 id="carbon">The carbon cycle</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Photosynthesis</strong> removes carbon dioxide from the air and turns it into organic compounds.
    <strong>Feeding</strong> passes them along food chains. <strong>Respiration</strong> by plants, animals and
    decomposers returns carbon dioxide to the air, and <strong>decomposition</strong> releases the carbon in dead matter.
    Some carbon is stored for a long time in wood, soils, oceans and fossil fuels; burning fossil fuels releases it much
    faster than it was stored.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nitrogen">The nitrogen cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Organisms need nitrogen to make proteins and DNA, but most cannot use the nitrogen gas that makes up 78% of the air.
    <strong>Nitrogen-fixing bacteria</strong>, some living in the root nodules of plants such as peas and clover, turn it
    into ammonium. <strong>Decomposers</strong> release ammonium from dead matter, <strong>nitrifying bacteria</strong>
    convert it to nitrates, and plants absorb nitrates through their roots. <strong>Denitrifying bacteria</strong> return
    some nitrogen to the air, and nitrates can also be washed out of soil by rain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Adding and removing organisms</h2>
  <p>
    Because organisms are linked, a change in one population spreads through the web. Remove a predator and its prey may
    increase until their food runs short; remove the prey and the predator declines. Remove the decomposers and nutrients
    stop cycling, so producers and everything that depends on them decline. Reducing the energy input, for example by
    shading, shrinks every level.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <p>
    Farmers rotate crops with legumes to add nitrogen to soil, and composting relies on decomposers. Fisheries scientists
    use energy transfer to estimate how many fish an ecosystem can support. Conservationists study food webs to predict the
    effects of losing or reintroducing species, such as wolves in Yellowstone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Energy is recycled by decomposers.”</strong> Decomposers recycle nutrients; the energy ends up as heat.</li>
    <li><strong>“Arrows in a food chain show who eats whom.”</strong> They show the direction energy moves: from food to feeder.</li>
    <li><strong>“Exactly 10% is passed on.”</strong> The share varies widely between ecosystems.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Energy flows one way through ecosystems and is lost as heat; matter is recycled.</li>
    <li>Producers capture energy, consumers obtain it by eating, and decomposers return nutrients.</li>
    <li>Only a small, variable share of energy passes from one trophic level to the next.</li>
    <li>Carbon and nitrogen cycle between the air, soil and living things, driven largely by microbes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">
    Model note: this is a simplified meadow with six groups of organisms and nitrogen as the limiting nutrient. All
    amounts are relative units chosen for teaching, not measurements, and no number in it should be taken as a constant
    for real ecosystems.
  </p>
</SimulatorLayout>
